{"name":"Bamboo Leaf","hex":"#99b243","fetched":"2026-10-11","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"bamboo","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Any of the fast-growing grasses of the Bambusoideae subfamily, characterized by its woody, hollow, round, straight, jointed stem.","Wood of the bamboo plant as a material for building, furniture, etc."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Made of the wood of the bamboo."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/bamboo"},{"term":"leaf","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The usually green and flat organ that represents the most prominent feature of most vegetative plants.","A foliage leaf or any of the many and often considerably different structures it can specialise into."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To produce leaves; put forth foliage.","To divide (a vegetable) into separate leaves."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/leaf"}],"entries":[{"title":"Bamboo","blocks":[{"text":"Bamboos are a diverse group of mostly evergreen perennial flowering plants making up the subfamily Bambusoideae of the grass family Poaceae. Giant bamboos are the largest members of the grass family, in the case of Dendrocalamus sinicus having individual stalks (culms) reaching a length of 46 meters (151 ft), up to 36 centimeters (14 in) in diameter and a weight of up to 450 kilograms (1,000 lb). It grows in tropical and subtropical regions, especially in Asia. The internodes of bamboos can also be of great length. Kinabaluchloa wrayi has internodes up to 2.5 meters (8 ft) in length, and Arthrostylidium schomburgkii has internodes up to 5 meters (16 ft) in length, exceeded in length only by papyrus. By contrast, the stalks of the tiny bamboo Raddiella vanessiae of the savannas of French Guiana measure only 10–20 millimeters (0.4–0.8 in) in length by about 2 millimeters (0.08 in) in width. The origin of the word \"bamboo\" is uncertain, but it most likely comes from the Dutch or Portuguese language, which originally borrowed it from Malay."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f3/Bamboo_forest.jpg/330px-Bamboo_forest.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bamboo","wikidata":"Q670887"},{"title":"Leaf","blocks":[{"text":"A leaf is a principal appendage of the stem of a vascular plant, usually borne laterally above ground and specialized for photosynthesis. Leaves are collectively called foliage, as in \"autumn foliage\", while the leaves, stem, flower, and fruit collectively form the shoot system. In most leaves, the primary photosynthetic tissue is the palisade mesophyll and is located on the upper side of the blade or lamina of the leaf, but in some species, including the mature foliage of Eucalyptus, palisade mesophyll is present on both sides and the leaves are said to be isobilateral. The leaf is an integral part of the stem system, and most leaves are flattened and have distinct upper (adaxial) and lower (abaxial) surfaces that differ in color, hairiness, the number of stomata, the amount and structure of epicuticular wax, and other features. Leaves are mostly green in color due to the presence of a compound called chlorophyll, which is essential for photosynthesis as it absorbs light energy from the Sun. A leaf with lighter-colored or white patches or edges is called a variegated leaf."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c7/Lisc_lipy.jpg/330px-Lisc_lipy.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Leaf","wikidata":"Q33971"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/84/Big_Bamboo_Shoot_%28Joi_Ito%29.jpg/500px-Big_Bamboo_Shoot_%28Joi_Ito%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/84/Big_Bamboo_Shoot_%28Joi_Ito%29.jpg/500px-Big_Bamboo_Shoot_%28Joi_Ito%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Fresh bamboo shoot emerging from the soil. These young, tender culms of bamboo plants are also widely harvested for culinary use in Asia.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ABig_Bamboo_Shoot_(Joi_Ito).jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/97/2021_Sagano_Bamboo_forest_in_Arashiyama%2C_Kyoto%2C_Japan.jpg/500px-2021_Sagano_Bamboo_forest_in_Arashiyama%2C_Kyoto%2C_Japan.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/97/2021_Sagano_Bamboo_forest_in_Arashiyama%2C_Kyoto%2C_Japan.jpg/960px-2021_Sagano_Bamboo_forest_in_Arashiyama%2C_Kyoto%2C_Japan.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/97/2021_Sagano_Bamboo_forest_in_Arashiyama%2C_Kyoto%2C_Japan.jpg/500px-2021_Sagano_Bamboo_forest_in_Arashiyama%2C_Kyoto%2C_Japan.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3A2021_Sagano_Bamboo_forest_in_Arashiyama%2C_Kyoto%2C_Japan.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/31/Trilha_do_Parque_Nacional_da_Serra_dos_%C3%93rg%C3%A3os_Sede_Petr%C3%B3polis.jpg/250px-Trilha_do_Parque_Nacional_da_Serra_dos_%C3%93rg%C3%A3os_Sede_Petr%C3%B3polis.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/31/Trilha_do_Parque_Nacional_da_Serra_dos_%C3%93rg%C3%A3os_Sede_Petr%C3%B3polis.jpg/330px-Trilha_do_Parque_Nacional_da_Serra_dos_%C3%93rg%C3%A3os_Sede_Petr%C3%B3polis.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/31/Trilha_do_Parque_Nacional_da_Serra_dos_%C3%93rg%C3%A3os_Sede_Petr%C3%B3polis.jpg/250px-Trilha_do_Parque_N